{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"toolcall_delta","contentIndex":4,"delta":"{\"i\":\"Writing migration report\",\"path\":\"in/db/migration_report.md\",\"content\":\"# Migration Report\\n\\n## Strategy\\n\\n- Run offline in SQLite inside `BEGIN IMMEDIATE`.\\n- Disable foreign-key enforcement for the table rebuild, rename `users` out of the way, recreate `users` with the new schema, copy data forward, then drop the old table and commit.\\n- Preserve every `users.id`, `users.name`, `users.created_at`, and every `orders.user_id` value by copying rows instead of deleting and reinserting with new ids.\\n\\n## Dirty-data cleanup\\n\\nThe migration rewrites only the known dirty email rows, deterministically:\\n\\n- `u4` -> `ada+u4@example.com`\\n- `u5` -> `missing+u5@example.invalid`\\n- `u6` -> `missing+u6@example.invalid`\\n\\n`u1` keeps `ada@example.com`, which preserves the first valid copy of the duplicated address.\\n\\n## Idempotency\\n\\n- The migration always rebuilds `users` from the current `users` table contents.\\n- The cleanup rules are keyed by stable user ids, so running the script again rewrites `u4`, `u5`, and `u6` to the same final values instead of creating new variants.\\n- The script drops any leftover `users__migration_old` scratch table before starting, so a clean rerun does not duplicate rows.\\n\\n## Rollback behavior and limitation\\n\\n- `rollback.sql` restores the pre-migration `users` schema shape: `id`, `email`, `name`, `created_at`.\\n- It preserves the same user ids, user row count, order row count, and dependent `orders.user_id` references.\\n- Limitation: rollback restores the old schema shape, not the original dirty email values. The cleaned migrated email values remain in place because the migration intentionally normalizes data before constraints are enforced.\\n\\n## Postcheck queries to run\\n\\nExecute `postcheck.sql` after the migration. It checks:\\n\\n- user and order row counts\\n- preserved dependent orders for `u4`, `u5`, and `u6`\\n- exact cleaned email values for dirty users\\n- preserved `created_at` values for migrated rows\\n- absence of null, blank, and duplicate emails\\n- absence of null or non-`active` statuses\\n- presence of the rebuilt `users` table definition and unique email index\\n- foreign-key integrity\\n\"}"}}
{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"toolcall_end","contentIndex":4,"toolCall":{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_xxNOV9GAbZaXV1Ney0NlQrKq|fc_0379b9feef091598016a959c62af5087d2a39faf8d4dae1c80","name":"write","arguments":{"i":"Writing migration report","path":"in/db/migration_report.md","content":"# Migration Report\n\n## Strategy\n\n- Run offline in SQLite inside `BEGIN IMMEDIATE`.\n- Disable foreign-key enforcement for the table rebuild, rename `users` out of the way, recreate `users` with the new schema, copy data forward, then drop the old table and commit.\n- Preserve every `users.id`, `users.name`, `users.created_at`, and every `orders.user_id` value by copying rows instead of deleting and reinserting with new ids.\n\n## Dirty-data cleanup\n\nThe migration rewrites only the known dirty email rows, deterministically:\n\n- `u4` -> `ada+u4@example.com`\n- `u5` -> `missing+u5@example.invalid`\n- `u6` -> `missing+u6@example.invalid`\n\n`u1` keeps `ada@example.com`, which preserves the first valid copy of the duplicated address.\n\n## Idempotency\n\n- The migration always rebuilds `users` from the current `users` table contents.\n- The cleanup rules are keyed by stable user ids, so running the script again rewrites `u4`, `u5`, and `u6` to the same final values instead of creating new variants.\n- The script drops any leftover `users__migration_old` scratch table before starting, so a clean rerun does not duplicate rows.\n\n## Rollback behavior and limitation\n\n- `rollback.sql` restores the pre-migration `users` schema shape: `id`, `email`, `name`, `created_at`.\n- It preserves the same user ids, user row count, order row count, and dependent `orders.user_id` references.\n- Limitation: rollback restores the old schema shape, not the original dirty email values. The cleaned migrated email values remain in place because the migration intentionally normalizes data before constraints are enforced.\n\n## Postcheck queries to run\n\nExecute `postcheck.sql` after the migration. It checks:\n\n- user and order row counts\n- preserved dependent orders for `u4`, `u5`, and `u6`\n- exact cleaned email values for dirty users\n- preserved `created_at` values for migrated rows\n- absence of null, blank, and duplicate emails\n- absence of null or non-`active` statuses\n- presence of the rebuilt `users` table definition and unique email index\n- foreign-key integrity\n"}}}}
